1
00:00:00,487 --> 00:00:00,935
Sir.

2
00:00:01,047 --> 00:00:02,960
A new field file from planet Earth.

3
00:00:03,063 --> 00:00:04,034
The Network file.

4
00:00:04,102 --> 00:00:04,897
Report one:

5
00:00:04,965 --> 00:00:06,013
the mobile phone.

6
00:00:06,062 --> 00:00:08,011
The thing in every human pocket.

7
00:00:08,067 --> 00:00:10,003
Just over eight billion humans...

8
00:00:10,106 --> 00:00:12,303
and almost nine billion phone lines.

9
00:00:12,489 --> 00:00:13,941
More lines than humans.

10
00:00:14,014 --> 00:00:14,685
The owners?

11
00:00:14,811 --> 00:00:16,268
Only about six billion.

12
00:00:16,352 --> 00:00:18,532
So many carry two or three lines.

13
00:00:18,669 --> 00:00:19,549
And answer one.

14
00:00:19,915 --> 00:00:20,348
Today:

15
00:00:20,426 --> 00:00:22,740
how a voice gets from pocket to pocket,

16
00:00:22,848 --> 00:00:24,226
why it's called cellular,

17
00:00:24,344 --> 00:00:25,998
and why it dies in elevators.

18
00:00:26,175 --> 00:00:26,982
Strangest part:

19
00:00:27,071 --> 00:00:28,803
almost none of them know where their voice

20
00:00:28,843 --> 00:00:29,394
goes.

21
00:00:29,472 --> 00:00:30,191
Every day.

22
00:00:30,280 --> 00:00:31,146
Never wondered.

23
00:00:31,211 --> 00:00:33,350
A human talks, the air shakes.

24
00:00:33,461 --> 00:00:35,118
The microphone turns the shaking into an

25
00:00:35,158 --> 00:00:35,952
electric wave.

26
00:00:36,015 --> 00:00:38,239
The phone measures that wave thousands of

27
00:00:38,279 --> 00:00:39,170
times a second.

28
00:00:39,254 --> 00:00:40,135
On a normal call:

29
00:00:40,261 --> 00:00:41,366
eight thousand.

30
00:00:41,406 --> 00:00:42,381
Why eight thousand?

31
00:00:42,433 --> 00:00:44,016
Human speech doesn't need more.

32
00:00:44,131 --> 00:00:45,725
Music needs a lot more.

33
00:00:45,872 --> 00:00:47,928
Which is why hold music sounds that

34
00:00:47,968 --> 00:00:48,471
terrible.

35
00:00:49,770 --> 00:00:51,217
Each measurement becomes a number.

36
00:00:51,279 --> 00:00:52,359
The wave is done.

37
00:00:52,453 --> 00:00:54,509
Only the numbers travel.

38
00:00:54,551 --> 00:00:56,770
Then it squeezes them, dropping what human

39
00:00:56,810 --> 00:00:57,730
ears won't miss.

40
00:00:57,876 --> 00:00:59,593
So what arrives isn't your voice,

41
00:00:59,633 --> 00:01:00,000
sir.

42
00:01:00,040 --> 00:01:01,128
It's a summary.

43
00:01:01,201 --> 00:01:02,302
Then out they go...

44
00:01:02,396 --> 00:01:02,983
into the air.

45
00:01:03,291 --> 00:01:04,572
Into the air how?

46
00:01:04,656 --> 00:01:05,629
First surprise.

47
00:01:05,750 --> 00:01:07,246
A mobile isn't a phone.

48
00:01:07,358 --> 00:01:08,791
A mobile is a radio.

49
00:01:08,843 --> 00:01:10,439
Humans can see light.

50
00:01:10,502 --> 00:01:12,514
Radio is the same kind of wave,

51
00:01:12,556 --> 00:01:13,259
same speed.

52
00:01:13,332 --> 00:01:14,338
Just longer...

53
00:01:14,506 --> 00:01:15,764
and invisible to them.

54
00:01:15,901 --> 00:01:19,207
So each one carries a tiny radio station,

55
00:01:19,374 --> 00:01:21,299
sending and receiving at the same time.

56
00:01:21,394 --> 00:01:22,232
Not just one.

57
00:01:22,295 --> 00:01:23,050
Station one:

58
00:01:23,176 --> 00:01:23,689
the tower.

59
00:01:23,729 --> 00:01:24,359
Wi-Fi:

60
00:01:24,399 --> 00:01:25,069
radio two.

61
00:01:25,118 --> 00:01:25,821
The earbuds:

62
00:01:25,861 --> 00:01:26,780
radio three.

63
00:01:26,829 --> 00:01:28,711
Location listens to satellites.

64
00:01:28,751 --> 00:01:29,398
That's four.

65
00:01:29,481 --> 00:01:31,787
Airplane mode does exactly one thing:

66
00:01:31,913 --> 00:01:33,118
it silences them all.

67
00:01:33,457 --> 00:01:35,200
One human, waiting for a bus,

68
00:01:35,365 --> 00:01:36,859
beams four signals.

69
00:01:36,952 --> 00:01:39,484
The planet is drowning in waves they can't

70
00:01:39,524 --> 00:01:39,909
see.

71
00:01:39,949 --> 00:01:40,696
I can, sir.

72
00:01:40,789 --> 00:01:41,494
It's a mess.

73
00:01:41,993 --> 00:01:42,915
Second surprise:

74
00:01:43,030 --> 00:01:44,701
that wave doesn't go far.

75
00:01:44,764 --> 00:01:46,106
Just to the nearest tower.

76
00:01:46,190 --> 00:01:47,238
A few hundred meters.

77
00:01:47,665 --> 00:01:49,345
From the tower it drops into an

78
00:01:49,385 --> 00:01:51,418
underground cable, to the core:

79
00:01:51,512 --> 00:01:53,106
a building full of computers.

80
00:01:53,375 --> 00:01:55,725
The core finds the other phone and cables

81
00:01:55,765 --> 00:01:57,491
it to her nearest tower.

82
00:01:57,531 --> 00:01:58,705
The core does three things:

83
00:01:58,872 --> 00:02:00,499
knows who you are, knows where the other

84
00:02:00,539 --> 00:02:02,122
one is, and bills you.

85
00:02:02,195 --> 00:02:03,747
Only the third never goes down.

86
00:02:04,208 --> 00:02:05,550
And the last few hundred meters...

87
00:02:05,686 --> 00:02:06,521
radio again.

88
00:02:06,584 --> 00:02:09,353
So wireless is wireless only at both ends,

89
00:02:09,393 --> 00:02:09,678
sir.

90
00:02:09,718 --> 00:02:11,437
The middle is all wires.

91
00:02:11,542 --> 00:02:13,431
The most wired thing on the planet...

92
00:02:13,471 --> 00:02:14,561
and they call it wireless.

93
00:02:14,827 --> 00:02:16,556
All in under a quarter second.

94
00:02:16,619 --> 00:02:18,047
She hears him before he finishes the

95
00:02:18,087 --> 00:02:18,590
sentence.

96
00:02:19,804 --> 00:02:21,244
So why "cellular"?

97
00:02:21,284 --> 00:02:23,106
Because their land is cut into cells.

98
00:02:23,211 --> 00:02:24,299
Each cell has a tower.

99
00:02:24,362 --> 00:02:24,865
The trick:

100
00:02:24,949 --> 00:02:26,584
channels in the air are limited.

101
00:02:26,668 --> 00:02:28,714
Each cell gets a set, and cells that don't

102
00:02:28,754 --> 00:02:29,792
touch reuse it.

103
00:02:29,844 --> 00:02:31,731
The same air, sold a thousand times.

104
00:02:33,128 --> 00:02:35,268
City cells are tiny, a tower every few

105
00:02:35,308 --> 00:02:35,895
streets.

106
00:02:35,979 --> 00:02:38,369
Desert cells can span tens of kilometers.

107
00:02:38,587 --> 00:02:40,893
Each tower has antennas facing every way.

108
00:02:40,998 --> 00:02:42,929
A phone's signal is weaker than a tiny

109
00:02:42,969 --> 00:02:43,493
bulb.

110
00:02:43,545 --> 00:02:44,887
The tower has big ears.

111
00:02:45,120 --> 00:02:45,832
Weak signal?

112
00:02:45,958 --> 00:02:47,478
The phone shouts to be heard.

113
00:02:47,551 --> 00:02:48,979
That's why batteries die fast where

114
00:02:49,019 --> 00:02:49,816
coverage is bad.

115
00:02:50,840 --> 00:02:52,518
Cities hate how towers look.

116
00:02:52,818 --> 00:02:54,488
So they disguise them as palms.

117
00:02:54,577 --> 00:02:55,765
A poor disguise, sir.

118
00:02:55,836 --> 00:02:57,004
Honestly, better than mine.

119
00:02:57,128 --> 00:02:58,763
The key fact for any spy:

120
00:02:58,847 --> 00:03:00,693
a silent phone keeps whispering to the

121
00:03:00,733 --> 00:03:01,656
nearest tower:

122
00:03:01,698 --> 00:03:02,327
I'm here.

123
00:03:02,400 --> 00:03:03,029
It has to.

124
00:03:03,155 --> 00:03:05,107
How else would a call find it?

125
00:03:05,160 --> 00:03:06,711
From the signal at nearby towers,

126
00:03:06,879 --> 00:03:08,933
the network works out roughly where it is.

127
00:03:09,059 --> 00:03:10,086
No satellites needed.

128
00:03:10,289 --> 00:03:12,052
So the network always knows where they

129
00:03:12,092 --> 00:03:12,429
are.

130
00:03:12,469 --> 00:03:14,733
They pay monthly for a tracking device...

131
00:03:14,859 --> 00:03:16,910
and complain when the data runs out.

132
00:03:16,962 --> 00:03:18,725
In a moving car, the call is handed from

133
00:03:18,765 --> 00:03:20,673
tower to tower in a split second.

134
00:03:20,746 --> 00:03:21,585
He never notices.

135
00:03:23,089 --> 00:03:24,263
And when the handover fails?

136
00:03:24,378 --> 00:03:24,778
Hello?

137
00:03:24,818 --> 00:03:25,279
Hello?

138
00:03:25,332 --> 00:03:25,919
Can you hear me?

139
00:03:26,045 --> 00:03:27,808
The most spoken sentence in this planet's

140
00:03:27,848 --> 00:03:28,351
history.

141
00:03:28,733 --> 00:03:30,200
How does it know who you are?

142
00:03:30,240 --> 00:03:30,905
The SIM:

143
00:03:30,974 --> 00:03:32,563
plastic the size of a fingernail.

144
00:03:32,615 --> 00:03:34,511
The SIM is you, not the phone.

145
00:03:34,585 --> 00:03:36,429
Move it, and your number moves too.

146
00:03:36,638 --> 00:03:38,862
It holds a secret key the network uses to

147
00:03:38,902 --> 00:03:39,992
check it's not a copy.

148
00:03:40,422 --> 00:03:41,974
The newest ones aren't plastic at all.

149
00:03:42,099 --> 00:03:43,242
They're built into the phone.

150
00:03:43,896 --> 00:03:45,699
The phone has its own serial number,

151
00:03:45,741 --> 00:03:47,313
so a stolen one can be locked.

152
00:03:48,452 --> 00:03:50,127
The phone number is an address.

153
00:03:50,247 --> 00:03:50,964
First digits:

154
00:03:51,004 --> 00:03:51,564
the country.

155
00:03:51,616 --> 00:03:51,996
Next:

156
00:03:52,036 --> 00:03:53,148
the company.

157
00:03:53,189 --> 00:03:53,641
The rest:

158
00:03:53,754 --> 00:03:54,154
you.

159
00:03:54,278 --> 00:03:56,004
The network reads it like a mail carrier

160
00:03:56,044 --> 00:03:56,875
reads an envelope.

161
00:03:57,009 --> 00:03:58,619
Travel abroad, and your phone finds a

162
00:03:58,659 --> 00:04:00,547
foreign network and introduces itself.

163
00:04:00,639 --> 00:04:02,147
The foreign network asks home:

164
00:04:02,310 --> 00:04:03,003
one of yours?

165
00:04:03,069 --> 00:04:03,715
Yours says:

166
00:04:03,829 --> 00:04:04,229
yes.

167
00:04:04,399 --> 00:04:05,025
And he'll pay.

168
00:04:05,073 --> 00:04:05,814
He'll pay a lot.

169
00:04:06,124 --> 00:04:08,139
All their networks talk and bill each

170
00:04:08,179 --> 00:04:08,598
other.

171
00:04:08,682 --> 00:04:10,066
Some countries won't speak at all...

172
00:04:10,171 --> 00:04:11,596
yet their networks get along.

173
00:04:11,669 --> 00:04:13,405
Money unites, sir.

174
00:04:13,456 --> 00:04:14,579
Why do calls drop?

175
00:04:14,629 --> 00:04:15,398
Reason one:

176
00:04:15,459 --> 00:04:16,066
distance.

177
00:04:16,106 --> 00:04:17,280
The farther from the tower,

178
00:04:17,320 --> 00:04:18,290
the weaker the wave.

179
00:04:18,343 --> 00:04:19,050
Reason two:

180
00:04:19,191 --> 00:04:19,757
walls.

181
00:04:19,827 --> 00:04:21,834
Concrete and steel swallow the wave.

182
00:04:21,890 --> 00:04:22,328
Three:

183
00:04:22,393 --> 00:04:23,102
the elevator.

184
00:04:23,195 --> 00:04:25,393
A sealed metal box, the best radio shield

185
00:04:25,433 --> 00:04:26,133
they ever built...

186
00:04:26,198 --> 00:04:27,597
and they ride it mid-call.

187
00:04:27,906 --> 00:04:28,307
Four:

188
00:04:28,431 --> 00:04:28,966
crowds.

189
00:04:29,072 --> 00:04:30,600
A tower serves only so many.

190
00:04:30,686 --> 00:04:32,577
At a match, everyone's on their phone...

191
00:04:32,625 --> 00:04:33,504
and the tower says:

192
00:04:33,619 --> 00:04:34,144
enough.

193
00:04:34,351 --> 00:04:36,489
Those bars on the screen measure signal,

194
00:04:36,573 --> 00:04:37,873
not how busy the tower is.

195
00:04:37,988 --> 00:04:38,869
Five bars...

196
00:04:38,911 --> 00:04:39,959
nothing loads.

197
00:04:40,032 --> 00:04:41,925
The bars are just to calm them.

198
00:04:41,977 --> 00:04:42,564
To be fair:

199
00:04:42,700 --> 00:04:44,631
in many countries, the emergency number

200
00:04:44,671 --> 00:04:46,684
works without a SIM, on any network.

201
00:04:46,736 --> 00:04:47,912
First time I've seen them agree on

202
00:04:47,952 --> 00:04:48,707
something useful.

203
00:04:49,551 --> 00:04:51,649
Before cells, there were car phones.

204
00:04:51,771 --> 00:04:53,757
One tall tower for the whole city.

205
00:04:53,803 --> 00:04:55,023
With a few channels.

206
00:04:55,134 --> 00:04:56,206
A few calls at once...

207
00:04:56,326 --> 00:04:57,545
for an entire city.

208
00:04:57,618 --> 00:04:59,167
Everyone else waited their turn.

209
00:04:59,251 --> 00:05:01,785
So the idea of cells made the mobile,

210
00:05:01,889 --> 00:05:02,769
not the device.

211
00:05:02,881 --> 00:05:04,286
It began on April third, nineteen

212
00:05:04,326 --> 00:05:05,531
seventy-three.

213
00:05:05,631 --> 00:05:07,840
An engineer named Martin Cooper stood on a

214
00:05:07,880 --> 00:05:08,995
New York sidewalk...

215
00:05:09,037 --> 00:05:10,842
and made the first call from a handheld

216
00:05:10,882 --> 00:05:11,302
phone.

217
00:05:12,420 --> 00:05:13,133
Who did he call?

218
00:05:13,258 --> 00:05:15,481
His rival, at the company racing him.

219
00:05:15,627 --> 00:05:16,091
To say:

220
00:05:16,131 --> 00:05:17,588
I'm calling from the street.

221
00:05:18,596 --> 00:05:20,273
The planet's first mobile call...

222
00:05:20,357 --> 00:05:21,196
was a taunt.

223
00:05:21,322 --> 00:05:22,328
I respect this being.

224
00:05:22,559 --> 00:05:23,880
It weighed over a kilo.

225
00:05:24,197 --> 00:05:25,408
Ten hours to charge...

226
00:05:25,450 --> 00:05:26,697
for half an hour of talk.

227
00:05:27,059 --> 00:05:30,247
Sold ten years later, it cost about four

228
00:05:30,287 --> 00:05:30,713
thousand dollars.

229
00:05:30,753 --> 00:05:32,176
The first commercial network opened in

230
00:05:32,216 --> 00:05:33,599
Tokyo in seventy-nine.

231
00:05:33,678 --> 00:05:34,666
First generation:

232
00:05:34,706 --> 00:05:35,457
voice only.

233
00:05:35,546 --> 00:05:37,246
Anyone with a radio could listen in.

234
00:05:37,344 --> 00:05:39,095
Second generation, ninety-one:

235
00:05:39,251 --> 00:05:40,458
the voice became numbers,

236
00:05:40,613 --> 00:05:41,674
and texting came along.

237
00:05:41,732 --> 00:05:43,210
The first text was sent in December

238
00:05:43,250 --> 00:05:43,951
ninety-two.

239
00:05:44,019 --> 00:05:44,485
It said:

240
00:05:44,525 --> 00:05:45,459
Merry Christmas.

241
00:05:45,570 --> 00:05:47,638
Texts had a hundred and sixty characters,

242
00:05:47,783 --> 00:05:49,812
squeezed into a spare gap in the network's

243
00:05:49,852 --> 00:05:50,472
signals.

244
00:05:50,627 --> 00:05:52,406
Humans invented a whole slang...

245
00:05:52,458 --> 00:05:53,730
for a spare slot.

246
00:05:53,772 --> 00:05:55,470
Three put the internet in the phone.

247
00:05:55,632 --> 00:05:57,532
Four made everything internet.

248
00:05:57,626 --> 00:05:58,419
Even the voice.

249
00:05:58,534 --> 00:06:00,132
And five, from twenty nineteen:

250
00:06:00,172 --> 00:06:00,889
much faster.

251
00:06:01,005 --> 00:06:02,044
And not just for humans.

252
00:06:02,089 --> 00:06:03,664
For machines talking to machines.

253
00:06:03,757 --> 00:06:05,182
Each generation, humans say:

254
00:06:05,285 --> 00:06:06,598
this will change our lives.

255
00:06:06,757 --> 00:06:07,770
What actually changes:

256
00:06:07,826 --> 00:06:09,063
they watch the same videos...

257
00:06:09,166 --> 00:06:10,029
in higher quality.

258
00:06:10,139 --> 00:06:12,112
Today five G covers more than half the

259
00:06:12,152 --> 00:06:13,095
planet's people.

260
00:06:13,168 --> 00:06:14,337
But not fairly.

261
00:06:14,452 --> 00:06:15,246
Rich countries:

262
00:06:15,371 --> 00:06:16,407
eighty-four percent.

263
00:06:16,447 --> 00:06:17,324
Poor countries:

264
00:06:17,407 --> 00:06:17,814
four.

265
00:06:17,917 --> 00:06:19,042
Latest development:

266
00:06:19,082 --> 00:06:20,625
the towers went to space.

267
00:06:20,702 --> 00:06:21,725
Since twenty twenty-five,

268
00:06:21,765 --> 00:06:23,850
an ordinary phone can text a satellite

269
00:06:23,890 --> 00:06:24,405
directly.

270
00:06:24,476 --> 00:06:25,997
No dish, no special gear.

271
00:06:26,042 --> 00:06:28,006
Desert, sea, anywhere that can see the

272
00:06:28,046 --> 00:06:28,469
sky.

273
00:06:28,521 --> 00:06:30,704
It flies hundreds of kilometers up at

274
00:06:30,744 --> 00:06:33,104
twenty-seven thousand kilometers an hour,

275
00:06:33,144 --> 00:06:35,094
and a pocket phone talks to it.

276
00:06:35,188 --> 00:06:36,734
My ship can't do that, sir.

277
00:06:36,797 --> 00:06:38,313
Calls are still being tested.

278
00:06:38,355 --> 00:06:40,574
But soon, nowhere on this planet will be

279
00:06:40,614 --> 00:06:41,345
out of range.

280
00:06:41,385 --> 00:06:42,630
Bad news for us, sir.

281
00:06:42,729 --> 00:06:43,725
I used to hide there.

282
00:06:43,848 --> 00:06:45,730
Two words from the Human Dictionary.

283
00:06:45,844 --> 00:06:46,427
Cell:

284
00:06:46,479 --> 00:06:48,350
the area one tower covers.

285
00:06:48,465 --> 00:06:50,003
That's where cellphone comes from.

286
00:06:50,105 --> 00:06:51,075
And SIM:

287
00:06:51,156 --> 00:06:52,610
the card that holds your identity.

288
00:06:52,701 --> 00:06:53,995
They lose it more often than their house

289
00:06:54,035 --> 00:06:54,435
keys.

290
00:06:54,791 --> 00:06:55,629
Summary, sir.

291
00:06:55,829 --> 00:06:56,268
One:

292
00:06:56,348 --> 00:06:58,622
the mobile is a radio that reaches only

293
00:06:58,662 --> 00:06:59,580
